This code computes the minimum path sum from the top-left corner to the bottom-right corner of a grid, where movement is only allowed downwards or rightwards. It uses a 2D DP table where dp[i][j] stores the minimum sum to reach cell (i,j). The base cases initialize the first row (dp[0][i] = grid[0][i] + dp[0][i-1]) and first column (dp[i][0] = grid[i][0] + dp[i-1][0]), since there's only one way to reach each. For all other cells, the recurrence dp[i][j] = grid[i][j] + min(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]) takes the smaller sum from either the top or left neighbor. Finally, dp[n-1][m-1] is returned.

Time Complexity: O(n × m) — iterates through all cells once.
Space Complexity: O(n × m) — for the DP table (can be optimized to O(m) with 1D DP).

class Solution {
    public int minPathSum(int[][] grid) {
        int n = grid.length;
        int m = grid[0].length;
        int[][] dp = new int[n][m];
        dp[0][0] = grid[0][0];
        for(int i = 1; i<n; i++){
            dp[i][0] = grid[i][0] + dp[i-1][0];
        } 
        for(int i = 1; i<m; i++){
            dp[0][i] = grid[0][i] + dp[0][i-1];
        }
        for(int i = 1; i<n; i++){
            for(int j = 1; j<m; j++){
                dp[i][j] = grid[i][j] + Math.min(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1]);
            }
        }
        return dp[n-1][m-1];
    }
}
